<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/15th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lam Sơn uprising</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1418">1418</a>–<a href="/years/1427">1427</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Lam Son uprising · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lam-son">Lam Sơn</a> uprising was the revolt led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-loi">Lê Lợi</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a>, which had occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> since 1407. Starting from Lam Sơn in Thanh Hóa, the rebels fought a long <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war and then besieged the Ming in Đông Quan. After the defeat of the Ming relief army at Chi Lăng in 1427 the Chinese withdrew, and Lê Lợi founded the Later Lê dynasty in 1428.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lam-son-uprising.jpg" alt="Lam Sơn uprising" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Leeaan · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Lam_S%C6%A1n_uprising_(en).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 1407 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a> annexed Đại Ngu, the short-lived state of the Hồ dynasty, and made it the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiaozhi">Jiaozhi</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yongle-emperor">Yongle Emperor</a> and courtiers such as Zhang Fu regarded the land as historical Chinese territory to be reclaimed. His policy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinicization">Sinicization</a>, with Chinese-style schools, Chinese-language examinations and northern settlers, provoked a series of uprisings. Most were put down, but the mountainous region of Thanh-Nghệ remained restless, and there <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-loi">Lê Lợi</a>, a hereditary local chief of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lam-son">Lam Sơn</a>, gathered followers and refused an official title offered to him by Huang Fu.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lam-son">Lam Sơn</a> lay only 20 km from the Ming base at the Tây Đô Citadel, and in its first years the rising was weak and suffered heavy losses. In 1423 a Ming and Lan Xang attack forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-loi">Lê Lợi</a> back to Chí Linh Mountain, where his men lived on bamboo shoots and he killed elephants and horses for food, and he then sought a truce. The death of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yongle-emperor">Yongle Emperor</a> on 12 August 1424 brought a milder policy under the Hongxi Emperor, with amnesties and lower taxes.</p><p>At a council in late 1424 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-loi">Lê Lợi</a> accepted Nguyễn Chích's plan to march on the lightly garrisoned province of Nghệ An. Within ten months the rebels held the land from Thanh Hóa to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuan-hoa">Thuận Hóa</a>. In late August and early September 1426 three divisions moved north; after defeating Wang Tong's counter-attack at Tốt Động – Chúc Động, Lê Lợi's forces struck the outposts of Đông Quan on the night of 22 November 1426 and shut the Ming inside. A relief expedition ordered by the Xuande Emperor under Liu Sheng and Mu Sheng was broken in the Battle of Chi Lăng – Xương Giang, where Liu Sheng and many senior generals were killed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Wang Tong agreed to negotiate, hostages were exchanged, and on 16 December 1427 both sides swore the Đông Quan oath, under which the Ming troops were allowed to withdraw safely. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-loi">Lê Lợi</a> at first kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tran-cao">Trần Cảo</a>, a purported descendant of the Trần dynasty, as a figurehead to win the Ming's trust; Trần Cảo died suddenly in February 1428, and some historians suspect Lê Lợi arranged his death. The scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-trai">Nguyễn Trãi</a> wrote the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/binh-ngo-dai-cao">Bình Ngô đại cáo</a> to proclaim the restoration of Viet autonomy, and the Later Lê dynasty lasted for nearly 400 years.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lam Sơn uprising” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It was the decisive battle of the <b>Lam Sơn uprising</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-7">7 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
